  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nBroad northwesterly flow aloft continues this afternoon thanks to \nthe positioning of a closed low over Hudson Bay and large scale \nridging over south-central CONUS. Small scale fluctuations in the \ngenerally northwestern flow could provide the impetus for scattered \nshowers and potentially a thunderstorm or two this afternoon and \nevening. Agreement among the CAMs has improved somewhat on timing \nand extent of showers for this afternoon.HREF SBCAPE forecasts for \nthis afternoon keep max values at or below 1500 J/kg, while the \nensemble means remain around 500 J/kg and the ensemble minimum keeps \nSBCAPE at 0 J/kg across the CWA. Shear remains weak and \nunidirectional. Thus, while SBCAPE may be sufficient to support a \ncouple of strong thunderstorms this afternoon, severe thunderstorms \nare not expected. HREF chances for showers producing at least a \ntenth of an inch of rain within 25 km of any point range from 20-40% \nthis afternoon and evening.The upper level pattern remains largely \nthe same throughout Wednesday afternoon, indicating that the \npotential for scattered showers and thunderstorms remains in the \nforecast for tomorrow. \n\nA broad 500mb ridging pattern is expected to move over eastern CONUS \nWednesday night into Thursday morning,indicating increasing surface \npressure and dry conditions Thursday and Friday. During the same \nperiod, a trough currently located over Seattle and Vancouver is \nlikely to progress eastward over Canada and northern CONUS. \nUncertainty remains regarding the precise timing, track, and depth \nof the trough, and thus its impacts for weekend conditions. At this \ntime, the highest chances (up to around 60%) for precipitation are \nexpected to occur between Saturday morning and Sunday morning, \nalthough given the uncertainty with trough timing and depth, the \nlocation, timing, and amount of precipitation is subject to change. \nGenerally northwesterly flow at the 500 mb is likely to resume \nSunday night through Tuesday as ridging returns to central CONUS, \ncontinuing the pattern of weak precipitation patterns over the UP.",
